                    What's new in PCBoard/UUCP 1.20
                    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  General Info
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~
  - PCBoard now supports up to 120 characters for subject lines.  When a
    person replies to a message online, they are prevented from editing
    the subject line.  Then, when UUOUT exports the message, it will
    prefix the subject with "Re: " if it isn't already there and will
    look up the original message id and copy it to the references header
    line.

  UUUTIL.EXE
  ~~~~~~~~~~
  - Support for FIDO has been removed from this UUCP program and added
    to PCBFUTIL, a new program included with PCBoard 15.22.

  UUOUT.EXE
  ~~~~~~~~~
  - Apologies to those who reported a bug in message-id/references
    header processing.  I've held fast for a while that it was working.
    I finally found what I think is the reported problem.  When UUIN
    imports a message, it takes the message id and stores it in a
    PCBoard extended header named UMSGID.  Ok so far.  Unfortunately,
    UUOUT was looking for a MSGID (without the U) extended header.  I
    believe this is now fixed and references should be working "more"
    correctly than they were previously.  Again, apologies for not
    seeing this before.

  - @LIST@ messages are now sent to each user specified and not just
    once to the (invalid) user @LIST@.  Before anyone asks, it is not
    possible to get more than 50 characters per address when using
    @LIST@, so if you need to send mail to an address longer than 50
    characters you'll have to do it with a separate message.

  UUXFER.EXE
  ~~~~~~~~~~
  - Some internal changes that are for the most part transparent.  The
    only thing that may be noticeable is that the program may use less
    time under multitasking environments (may, and not a lot) due to
    improved communications port buffering code.

  - Had several reports of program hangs in the protocol that would
    prevent UUXFER from timing out and disconnecting if the service
    provider gets stuck.  Apparently fixed.

  - Had several reports of program hangs with 2K and 4K block sizes.
    Apparently fixed.

  - NOTE:  Apparently fixed means that we were never able to reliably
    reproduce the problem in house and as a result, any fixes that
    appear to fix it haven't been rigorously tested.  However, it
    "appears" to have fixed the problems experienced by selected test
    sites.

  UUIN.EXE
  ~~~~~~~~
  - Multiple email conferences are now supported in the following manner:

      First, a conference with the internet user name is searched for
        and used if found (and the message will be forced public to
        ALL unless it's an email type conference in which case it will
        be forced private to ALL).

      Second, a conference named "site.email.l" is searched for and used
        if found (where site is the UUCP Name defined in PCBSetup and l
        is the first letter of their internet user name from A to Z [if
        the letter is less than A it will be adjusted up to A, and if
        greater than Z it will be adjusted down to Z]).

      Third, a conference named "site.email" is searched for and used if
        found.

      Finally, if none of the above resulted in a successful match, the
        default email conference defined in PCBSetup is used.

    For example, imagine email comes to site lcars for
    "david.letterman.topten.lists@lcars.com".  UUIN would try to post
    the message in the following manner:

      First, a conference named "david.letterman.topten.lists" would be
        searched for and used if found (and the message will be forced
        public to ALL).

      Second, if "david.letterman.topten.lists" didn't exist, a
        conference named "lcars.email.d" would be searched for and used
        if found.

      Third, if "lcars.email.d" didn't exist, a conference named
        "lcars.email" would be searched for and used if found.

      Finally, if "lcars.email" didn't exist, the email conference
        defined in PCBSetup would be used.

    The first lookup is useful for subscribing to a mail list for the
    entire system.  Merely subscribe under the conference name you wish
    to use for the mail list.  Set the conference type to 3 for
    Moderated Newsgroup and set the address of the moderator to the list
    address, if desired.

    The second lookup is useful for spreading email around multiple
    conferences based on the user name, keeping the traffic in a single
    conference to a minimum.

    The third lookup is used to provide a "default" email conference
    with the same name format as the second lookup.

    The fourth lookup is to provide backward compatibility with earlier
    versions of UUIN.

  - Added "infobot" support.  Simply add a line like this to your
    ALIAS.IN file:

      info,%c:\pcb\uucp\info.txt

    And any messages that come to info will be automatically replied to
    with the file (c:\pcb\uucp\info.txt) specified after the percent
    sign (%).  Multiple infobots may be set up if desired.

  - Added "auto forward" support.  It's like an alias that goes to a
    different site.  Add a line like this to your ALIAS.IN file:

      scott.robison,scott.robison@lcars.com

    And define a file named FORWARD.TXT in your Base UUCP directory
    (similar to the BOUNCE.TXT file).  When a message comes in to
    scott.robison, it will be forwarded to the address to the right of
    the comma.  FORWARD.TXT should include information you wish to give
    to the person receiving the forwarded message, such as instructions
    on informing contacts of their new address.

  - All headers are imported regardless of the lines specified in
    HDRKEYS.LST.  However, the lines in HDRKEYS.LST will be hidden.
    They are stored so that they information contained therein may be
    accessed later by programs which know how to access it.

  - Added -w switch to UUIN to allow the number of days to hang on to
    incomplete multipart messages to be specified.  The default is 7 (as
    now).  To change it, use it like this:

       uuin -s site -w 3

    That command line would only hold onto incomplete mail for 3 days.

  - In conjunction with the -w switch, incomplete messages will now be
    imported as plain text instead of being thrown away at the end of
    the wait period to ensure no data loss.

  - Whenever a message is automatically handled by UUIN, the original
    message will be sent in it's entirety to the recipient of the
    auto-reply.  This will help them in knowing why they received an
    auto-reply.

